{
  "title": "The 30+ best things to do in Tokyo with kids (2026) - Globetotting",
  "url": "https://globetotting.com/things-to-do-with-kids-in-tokyo",
  "snippet": "The Global Family to Family community holds close to 100 child-friendly events every month in cities across Japan, including Tokyo, Kyoto and Osaka. Events include picnics in parks, zoo visits, BBQs and more, and they are open to local families and visitors alike and are a great way to meet other families – and get the inside track on the best things to do in Tokyo!\n\nFind out more information on their website.\n\nTokyo with Kids\n\nTake part in a Global Family to Family event in Tokyo\n\n7 day itinerary Tokyo\n\nThe most popular stall at the Tsukiji Fish Market\n\n### Nezu Shrine\n\nNot far from Ueno Park is Nezu Shrine, less well known by tourists but it’s well worth visiting, especially if your family vacation coincides with azalea season. [...] Takeshita Street gets busy at the weekends so be prepared for crowds! Of course, being Japan, the crowds are ordered and polite with police on the scene carrying signs urging people to “keep left” when walking down the street.\n\n### Yoyogi-koen Park\n\nNot far from Takeshita Street is Yoyogi Park, one of the largest parks in Tokyo. If you’re looking for a break from the concrete and bright lights then Yoyogi Park is a great place to come. That said, this park is hugely popular so it does get lively.\n\nYoyogi Park officially became a public park in 1967. Prior to that it was a military parade ground, home to US military barracks when the Americans occupied Japan and was also where the main athlete’s village was housed during the 1964 Tokyo Olympics. [...] The other reason to visit Ueno Park is for the cherry blossoms. More than 1,000 cherry blossom trees line the park’s central pathway making it a popular destination for hanami, the traditional Japanese art of cherry blossom viewing.\n\n### Meet local families and take part in kid-friendly events\n\nOne of the best ways to get to know a place is by meeting locals. They are the ones who can tell you where you should go for lunch, share top tips on how to snag tickets to the Ghibli Museum and guide you on the best sights and attractions. The good news is that in Tokyo, there is a group of families that can do just this."
}